+## Deploying a single-host installation
+
+The repository includes a Kamal configuration for running the SQLite edition of Fizzy
+on one Linux server. The application, SQLite databases, local uploads, and Solid Queue
+all run in the same container.
+
+1. Point a DNS record at the server and make sure SSH access is available. Docker must
+   be available on both the local machine and the server.
+2. Edit the `app_fqdn` value near the top of `config/deploy.yml` and replace
+   `smtp.example.com` with the SMTP server for the installation.
+3. Fill in the six values in `.kamal/secrets`. `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` and
+   `SECRET_KEY_BASE` should be generated or copied from a secure password manager.
+   Generate the VAPID key pair as described in [Web Push Notifications](#web-push-notifications).
+4. Run:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+Kamal uses its local registry and forwards it over SSH, so a separate hosted registry
+is not required. `kamal-proxy` obtains the HTTPS certificate; ports 80 and 443 must be
+reachable from the internet for the certificate challenge and redirects.
+
+The `fizzy:/rails/storage` Docker volume is deliberately separate from each deployed
+container. It holds all SQLite databases and local Active Storage files, so deploying a
+new image does not replace the application data. Do not remove that volume when
+cleaning up the server.
+
+
